==Russian==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|[got]}}

===Etymology===
Old Russian '''годъ''' (time, period) < [[OCS]] '''[[годъ]]''' (pleasant time) < PIE base '''*gʰedʰ-''' (to unite, to be associated, to be suitable). Cognates include Ukrainian [[годi]] (enough), Bulgarian [[годе]] ([[ever]], as in [[кой-годе]] = whoever), Czech [[hod#Czech|hod]], Polish [[gody]] (feast), Upper Sorbian [[hody]] (Christmas), Lower Sorbian [[gódy]] (Christmas), Latvian [[gads]] (year), German [[Gatte]] (husband) and [[gätlich]] (convenient), as well as English [[gather]] and [[good]], Dutch [[goed]], and German [[gut#German|gut]]. Descendants include Russian [[погода]], [[выгода]], [[угода]], [[годный]], and [[годиться]].

===Noun===
'''год''' (god) {{m}}, [[годы]] (gódy) {{p}}, [[года]] (godá) {{p}}
:'''''Usage note:''' Use the appropriate case of '''''год''''' after simple or compound numbers ending in ''[[один]]'' (1), ''[[два]]'' (2), ''[[три]]'' (3), or ''[[четыре]]'' (4), except for 11, 12, 13 and 14 (and 111 - 114, 211 - 214 etc.) - for these and all other numbers, and with [[adverb]]s of quantity (''[[много]]'', ''[[сколько]]'', etc.), use the appropriate case of '''''[[лето]]'''.

#[[year]]
#:*{{RUchar|[[в#Russian|в]] [[этот|э́том]] году́}} — ''this year''
#:*{{RUchar|года́ми}} — ''for years; for years on end''
#:*{{RUchar|[[девяностый|девяно́стые]] го́ды}} — ''the ’90s''
#:*{{RUchar|[[учебный|уче́бный]] год}} — ''school year; academic year''
#:*{{RUchar|[[новый|Но́вый]] год}} — ''New Year’s Day''
#:*{{RUchar|[[он|Ему́]] [[двадцать один|два́дцать оди́н]] год}} — ''He is 21 years old.''
#:*{{RUchar|[[он|Ему́]] [[двадцать три|два́дцать три]] го́да}} — ''He is 23 years old.''
#:*{{RUchar|[[он|Ему́]] [[двадцать пять|два́дцать пять]] [[лето|лет]]}} — ''He is 25 years old.''

